\title{G\"odel's Casino: A Game-Theoretic Analysis of Betting on Undecidable Statements}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce and rigorously analyze \emph{G\"odel's Casino}, a betting game in which a
player wagers on the truth value of statements --- including statements that are
independent of a background theory in the sense of holding in some models and failing in
others. Motivated by the provocative conjecture that undecidability can be monetized (that
each undecidable statement is individually winnable with strictly positive expected value,
subject to a universal per-round lower bound of $1/3$), we build a fully self-contained
finite model and settle the conjecture in the negative. We prove that the game is zero-sum
with no house edge; that decidable statements (valid or unsatisfiable) are winnable with
maximal expected profit $1$; that any \emph{independent} statement suffers a worst-case
payoff of $-1$ regardless of the bet; and, decisively, that a \emph{balanced} statement
(true in exactly half the worlds) yields expected profit exactly $0$ for every bet. We
exhibit an explicit independent statement on which no bet has positive expected value,
refuting the individual-winnability claim, and a deck of balanced cards whose average
optimal profit is $0$, refuting the $1/3$ bound. The honest positive theory is that
optimal profit is nonnegative, that a deck of decidable cards is won every round, and that
a mixed deck with a fraction $f$ of decidable cards has average optimal profit exactly
$f$. The player's entire edge derives from the decidable fragment; genuine incompleteness
contributes exactly zero in expectation and $-1$ in the worst case. In this precise
game-theoretic sense, incompleteness is a barrier, not a free lunch.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

G\"odel's incompleteness theorems and the subsequent independence results of Cohen and
others established that formal mathematics is permanently haunted by statements it can
neither prove nor refute. The Continuum Hypothesis is the canonical example: it is
\emph{independent} of the standard axioms, holding in some models of set theory and
failing in others. A recurring popular intuition holds that this multiplicity of models is
not merely a limitation but a resource --- that if a statement is ``right in some model,''
then a gambler betting on its truth ought to be able to profit.

This paper takes that intuition seriously and tests it inside a precise decision-theoretic
model. We formalize a betting game --- \emph{G\"odel's Casino} --- and evaluate a specific
optimistic conjecture drawn from the informal literature.

\begin{conjecture}[Monetizable Undecidability]
There is a betting strategy on statements independent of a theory that guarantees strictly
positive expected profit, with each undecidable bet individually winnable and a universal
lower bound of $1/3$ expected profit per round.
\end{conjecture}

Our contribution is to show that this conjecture is \emph{false}, to prove exactly which
parts of the intuition survive, and to isolate the source of any genuine edge. The
analysis is elementary and self-contained: it requires only finite sums of rational
numbers and does not depend on any formalization of the arithmetic hierarchy or of set
theory. Its purpose is to capture the \emph{game-theoretic content} of the conjecture
faithfully, and in that arena the verdict is unambiguous.

\section{The Model}

\subsection{Worlds, statements, and bets}

Fix a finite, nonempty set $\Omega$ of \emph{worlds} (intuitively, the models of the
background theory). A \textbf{statement} is a function $s : \Omega \to \{\text{true},
\text{false}\}$, recording the truth value of $s$ in each world. A \textbf{bet} is an
element $b \in \{\text{true}, \text{false}\}$. For betting purposes a statement is exactly
its pattern of truth values across worlds, and nothing more.

\subsection{Payoffs}

The per-world \textbf{payoff} of betting $b$ on statement $s$ in world $\omega$ is
\[
\payoff(s, b, \omega) =
\begin{cases}
+1 & \text{if } b = s(\omega), \\
-1 & \text{if } b \neq s(\omega).
\end{cases}
\]
This is a scrupulously symmetric, fair-odds rule: a correct call earns one unit, an
incorrect call loses one.

\subsection{Two evaluations of a bet}

Under the uniform prior over $\Omega$, the \textbf{expected profit} of a bet is
\[
\expP(s, b) = \frac{1}{|\Omega|} \sum_{\omega \in \Omega} \payoff(s, b, \omega).
\]
Against an adversarial house that reveals the least favorable world, the
\textbf{worst-case profit} is $\min_{\omega} \payoff(s, b, \omega)$.

\subsection{Classes of statements}

Let $\trueCount(s) = |\{\omega \in \Omega : s(\omega) = \text{true}\}|$. We distinguish:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $s$ is \textbf{valid} if $s(\omega) = \text{true}$ for all $\omega$ (a decidable truth);
  \item $s$ is \textbf{unsatisfiable} if $s(\omega) = \text{false}$ for all $\omega$ (a decidable falsehood);
  \item $s$ is \textbf{independent} if $s(\omega) = \text{true}$ for some $\omega$ and $s(\omega') = \text{false}$ for some $\omega'$;
  \item $s$ is \textbf{balanced} if $2\,\trueCount(s) = |\Omega|$ (true in exactly half the worlds).
\end{itemize}
Every balanced statement is independent; the converse fails.

\subsection{The optimal bet and decks}

The \textbf{optimal expected profit} of a statement is
\[
\optP(s) = \max\bigl(\expP(s, \text{true}),\ \expP(s, \text{false})\bigr).
\]
A \textbf{deck} is a finite list $D = [s_1, \dots, s_n]$; the deck's average optimal
profit is $\deckP(D) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \optP(s_i)$.

\section{Structural Results}

\begin{lemma}[Pointwise zero-sum]
For every statement $s$ and world $\omega$,
$\payoff(s, \text{true}, \omega) + \payoff(s, \text{false}, \omega) = 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In each world exactly one of the two bets matches $s(\omega)$, paying $+1$, while the
other pays $-1$. Both cases give $(+1) + (-1) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-sum game]
For every statement $s$, $\expP(s, \text{true}) + \expP(s, \text{false}) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Summing the previous lemma over all worlds gives $0$; dividing by $|\Omega|$ preserves it.
\end{proof}

The casino has no built-in edge in either direction. Any positive expected profit a player
achieves must arise entirely from information about the card.

\begin{lemma}[Total TRUE-payoff]
For every statement $s$,
$\sum_{\omega \in \Omega} \payoff(s, \text{true}, \omega) = 2\,\trueCount(s) - |\Omega|$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition $\Omega$ into true worlds (each contributing $+1$) and false worlds (each
contributing $-1$). There are $\trueCount(s)$ of the former and $|\Omega| - \trueCount(s)$
of the latter, giving $2\,\trueCount(s) - |\Omega|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Expected-profit formula]
For every statement $s$,
$\displaystyle \expP(s, \text{true}) = \frac{2\,\trueCount(s) - |\Omega|}{|\Omega|}$.
\end{theorem}

Expected profit on the TRUE bet is a strictly increasing affine function of the fraction of
worlds in which the statement holds: from $-1$ (never true) through $0$ (true in half the
worlds) to $+1$ (always true).

\section{Decidable Statements Are Winnable}

\begin{theorem}[Valid statements pay the maximum]
If $s$ is valid, then $\expP(s, \text{true}) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every payoff for the TRUE bet is $+1$; the average of a constant $1$ is $1$. Equivalently,
$\trueCount(s) = |\Omega|$ in the expected-profit formula.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unsatisfiable statements pay the maximum]
If $s$ is unsatisfiable, then $\expP(s, \text{false}) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Symmetric: every FALSE bet matches, so every payoff is $+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A valid statement has $\optP(s) = 1$, and by the zero-sum law the TRUE bet beats the FALSE
bet, which returns $-1$.
\end{corollary}

Decidable statements are perfectly winnable, and they are precisely the statements that are
not undecidable.

\section{Independence Cannot Be Beaten}

\begin{theorem}[Guaranteed worst-case loss on independent cards]
If $s$ is independent, then for every bet $b$ there exists a world $\omega$ with
$\payoff(s, b, \omega) = -1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Independence supplies $\omega_T$ with $s(\omega_T) = \text{true}$ and $\omega_F$ with
$s(\omega_F) = \text{false}$. If $b = \text{true}$, then $\omega_F$ pays $-1$; if
$b = \text{false}$, then $\omega_T$ pays $-1$.
\end{proof}

Thus the worst-case profit on any independent statement is $\leq -1 < 0$. Dually, every
independent card also has a winning world, but this offers no protection against an
adversary.

\begin{theorem}[Balanced statements have no edge]
If $s$ is balanced, then $\expP(s, b) = 0$ for every bet $b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Balancedness makes the numerator $2\,\trueCount(s) - |\Omega|$ vanish, so
$\expP(s, \text{true}) = 0$; the zero-sum law gives $\expP(s, \text{false}) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A balanced statement has $\optP(s) = 0$: it is not winnable even in the expected-value
sense. A balanced independent card is, in every measurable respect, a fair coin.
\end{corollary}

\section{Refuting the Conjecture}

\begin{theorem}[An explicit unwinnable independent card]
There exists a statement that is independent yet on which every bet has expected profit
exactly $0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $\Omega = \{\text{true}, \text{false}\}$ and let $s$ be the identity. Then $s$ is
independent (it takes both values) and balanced ($\trueCount(s) = 1 = |\Omega|/2$), so
every bet returns $0$.
\end{proof}

This is the miniature Continuum-Hypothesis card: ``right in some model, wrong in another.''
It is genuinely undecidable in the sense that matters, and it is worth nothing. This refutes
the claim that each undecidable statement is individually winnable with strictly positive
expected value.

\begin{theorem}[The $1/3$ bound fails]
There exists a nonempty deck $D$ with $\deckP(D) = 0$, hence $\deckP(D) < 1/3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $D$ consist of a single balanced card. Its optimal profit is $0$, so the deck average
is $0 < 1/3$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Honest Positive Theory}

\begin{theorem}[Optimal profit is nonnegative]
For every statement $s$, $\optP(s) \geq 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two bets sum to $0$, so at least one is $\geq 0$; the maximum is therefore $\geq 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Decidable decks are won every round]
If a nonempty deck $D$ consists entirely of valid statements, then $\deckP(D) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each card has optimal profit $1$; the average of constants equal to $1$ is $1$.
\end{proof}

More generally, a mixed deck in which a fraction $f$ of the cards are decidable (each
paying $1$) and the rest are balanced (each paying $0$) has average optimal profit exactly
$f$. Every unit of long-run profit is contributed by a decidable card.

\begin{theorem}[Soundness yields a real edge --- on decidable cards]
Let $\Prov$ be any predicate on statements such that $\Prov(s)$ implies $s$ is valid.
Then for any provable statement $s$, $\expP(s, \text{true}) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\Prov(s)$ holds then $s$ is valid by soundness, and the valid-statement theorem
applies.
\end{proof}

This is the rigorous form of ``bet TRUE on provable statements.'' It genuinely wins ---
but only because a provable statement is, by soundness, decidable-true, not independent.

\section{Discussion}

The results assemble into a single dichotomy for the value of a card:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Card type & Expected profit (optimal bet) & Worst-case profit \\
\midrule
Valid / unsatisfiable (decidable) & $+1$ & $+1$ \\
Balanced (independent) & $0$ & $-1$ \\
General independent & $\in [0, 1)$ & $-1$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The optimistic conjecture conflated the genuine winnability of decidable statements with a
supposed winnability of undecidable ones. Expected profit is governed solely by the
fraction of worlds in which a statement holds. Decidable statements sit at the extremes and
pay the maximum; independent statements sit strictly inside; balanced ones sit at the exact
center, worth nothing. No cleverness moves a card along this scale.

The verdict is: \emph{you can win at the decidable fragment; the undecidable fragment is
exactly a fair coin (expected $0$) or an adversarial loss ($-1$)}. Incompleteness, in this
precise game-theoretic sense, is a barrier and not a free lunch.

\section{Future Directions}

One may replace the uniform prior with an arbitrary probability measure, generalizing the
expected-profit formula to $2\,\mathbb{P}[s] - 1$ and shifting the balanced boundary to
$\mathbb{P}[s] = 1/2$. One may study sequential play in which revealed worlds constrain
future cards, connecting the casino to online learning and regret minimization. One may
allow fractional or hedged bets and continuous payoffs, recovering a proper scoring-rule
perspective. Finally, one may align the world-model with genuine proof-theoretic strength,
replacing ``valid'' with ``provable in a fixed sound theory'' and quantifying how each new
independent axiom migrates cards from the balanced center toward the winnable extremes.

\end{document}

